I've written the session plan: two task files and the issue responses, all in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The instructions end the commit command with || true, so I took that as expected and didn't force-add the files with -f.

Task 1: stop a pipe from getting one copy of the partial answer per retry (#976, prompt subsystem, `Kind: product`). This is the self-driven slot, and it's outside main, which the trajectory says took 4 of the last 8 self-driven changes. Text already written to a pipe can't be taken back, so the plan sets a rule up front: if stdout is not a terminal and the failing attempt has already printed answer text, don't retry. Exit nonzero and say on stderr why the retry was skipped. If the attempt failed before printing anything (usually the case for overload errors), it retries as today, and terminal behaviour doesn't change.
• Every retry site goes through one shared helper, so the two retry loops can't drift apart.
• The step-0 reproduction is the existing test, run unchanged, with its raw stdout bytes recorded.
• That test's contains check becomes an exact match, with a second test showing a retry before any output still works.
• The helper gets broken and restored in one command, to show the exact-match test fails without it.
• If the bug turns out not to reproduce, the task records that with the raw output rather than inventing a fix.

Task 2: tell stream-json callers when a session was restored (#979 half 2, `Kind: product`). This is the issue-driven slot. Directly after the first agentStart, stream-json output gets {"type":"sessionRestored","messages":N}. It appears once per process, and only when a restore actually happened. With no restore there is no line at all rather than messages: 0, so default output stays exactly as it is now. The tests check the line where it is emitted, plus the no-restore case and a second turn. Once it lands, #979 gets a comment quoting the line and is closed. Half 1 (--continue-strict) has already shipped.

